            <ArticleTitle>Preventing eccentric reaming of the trochanter during trochanteric nailing.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e88-90</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/BOT.0b013e3182a59c80</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The lateralizing vector of a flexible guide wire can result in eccentric lateral reaming of the proximal femur during intramedullary nailing procedures. This effect is especially true with peritrochanteric fractures that have a fracture line exit near the entry point, and in obese patients. We present one method of maintaining a co-axial position of the guide wire and reamer assembly to help direct the portal of reaming in a more anatomic position. Use of a concave shaped retractor such as an Appendiceal or Richardson helps to &quot;capture&quot; the reamer shaft and control where proximal reaming occurs. We have found this method to be easy to use and effective when indicated.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
